    <Header>
        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Headline" As="TextElement.H1">Testing</TbText>
        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" class="docs-lead">
            Pages built with Trailblazor are meant to be easy to test. One small library sets up bUnit, another drives
            components in Playwright, and every component can be found by its role and name, the way users find it.
        </TbText>
    </Header>
    <ChildContent>
        <DocsHeading Id="bunit" Text="Set up bUnit" Class="docs-section" />
        <ol>
            <li>Reference <code>Trailblazor.Testing</code> from the test project, once it's available to install.</li>
            <li>Register any services of your own, such as a <code>FakeTimeProvider</code>.</li>
            <li>
                Call <code>this.AddTrailblazor()</code> before anything renders, usually in the test class's
                constructor.
            </li>
            <li>Render components and use them as in any bUnit test.</li>
        </ol>
        <DocsCallout Tone="Tone.Warning" Title="Register your services first">
            bUnit accepts no new services once a component renders, a service is resolved or
            <code>SetRendererInfo</code> is called.
        </DocsCallout>
        <CodeBlock Label="A bUnit test" Code="@Bunit" />

        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" As="TextElement.H3">What AddTrailblazor sets up</TbText>
        <ul>
            <li>Trailblazor's services.</li>
            <li>
                Answers to its JavaScript calls, so components render and respond to clicks with no JSInterop setup. A
                call you set up yourself still wins.
            </li>
            <li>
                A <code>FakeBrowserService</code>, which records what was copied, downloaded and saved. The
                <a href="guides/browser">browser guide</a> shows it.
            </li>
            <li>
                A <code>FakeDialogService</code>, described under
                <a href="guides/testing#bunit-dialogs">Test dialogs in bUnit</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
                A <code>FakeAnnouncer</code>, described under
                <a href="guides/testing#announcements">Test what screen readers hear</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
                An interactive renderer, so components render as they do once a page is interactive, with no renderer
                setup.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" As="TextElement.H3">Static and prerendered pages</TbText>
        <TbText>
            A few components render differently before a page is interactive: a select, for example, renders a native
            <code>&lt;select&gt;</code>. Each component's page says how it differs. To test what a statically rendered
            or prerendered page shows, call this before rendering:
        </TbText>
        <CodeBlock Label="A static renderer" Code="@StaticRenderer" />

        <DocsHeading Id="bunit-fields" Text="Drive fields in bUnit" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>
            <code>cut.Tb()</code> finds fields by their labels and drives them the way a user does. A helper the
            <a href="guides/testing#playwright">Playwright helpers</a> also have goes by the same name there: a read
            such as <code>Text</code> here is <code>TextAsync()</code> there. A grid, tree, sortable list,
            pagination, query builder, viewport, tabs, context menu or command palette that's the only one of its
            kind can be found without its name, as in <code>Grid()</code>.
        </TbText>
        <div class="docs-api" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="bUnit helpers">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Helper</th>
                        <th scope="col">What it does</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>Select(label).ChooseAsync(options)</code></th>
                        <td>Chooses options by their text, keeping those already chosen.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>Select(label).Text</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Reads what the select shows, and <code>Chosen</code> reads the chosen options.
                            <code>Option(name)</code> reads whether an option is <code>Disabled</code> and
                            <code>Chosen</code>, and <code>Disabled</code> reads the select.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>Select(label).UnchooseAsync(options)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Unchooses options in a multiple select, and <code>ClearAsync()</code> clears a
                            <code>Clearable</code> select with its clear button.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>ListBox(label).ChooseAsync(options)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Chooses options in a list box by their text, and <code>Chosen</code> reads the chosen ones.
                            In a multiple list box, <code>UnchooseAsync(options)</code> unchooses some and
                            <code>ClearAsync()</code> unchooses all. <code>Option(name)</code> and
                            <code>Disabled</code> read an option and the list box, as for a select.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>TextField(label).EnterAsync(text)</code></th>
                        <td>Types text and commits it, and <code>Text</code> reads what the field shows.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>Within(() =&gt; element)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Finds components only inside an element, such as a grid's row, so a field that repeats in
                            every row can be told apart. It finds the element again each time a helper runs, so a render
                            doesn't leave it stale. In Playwright, <code>row.Tb()</code> does the same.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>NumberField(label).EnterAsync(text)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Types a number and waits until the app has it. <code>StepAsync(count)</code> clicks the
                            increase button that many times, or the decrease button for a negative count, and stops at
                            <code>Min</code> or <code>Max</code>.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>Checkbox(label).CheckAsync()</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Checks it, and <code>UncheckAsync</code> unchecks it; <code>Checked</code> reads it.
                            <code>Switch(label)</code> works the same way. Both refuse a read-only or disabled box.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>CheckboxGroup(label).CheckAsync(options)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Checks options by their text, <code>UncheckAsync</code> unchecks them, and
                            <code>Checked</code> reads the checked ones.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>RadioGroup(label).ChooseAsync(option)</code></th>
                        <td>Chooses an option by its text, and <code>Chosen</code> reads it.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>ToggleGroup(label).ChooseAsync(items)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Presses items by name, <code>UnchooseAsync</code> releases them, and <code>Chosen</code>
                            reads the pressed ones.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>List(name).ClickAsync(item)</code></th>
                        <td>Clicks an item by its text, and <code>Items</code> reads them.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>TagInput(label).AddAsync(text)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Adds a tag, or shows the field's message for one it refuses, and waits for the app's
                            answer. <code>RemoveAsync(tag)</code> removes one and waits too, and <code>Tags</code>
                            reads them.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>RichTextEditor(label).EnterAsync(html)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Replaces the editor's text with this HTML, keeping only the formats it allows, and waits
                            until the app has it.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>TransferList(label)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Moves items to the chosen list with <code>MoveAsync</code> and back with
                            <code>MoveBackAsync</code>, and reads the chosen ones with <code>Chosen</code>.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>Calendar(label).ChooseAsync(date)</code></th>
                        <td>Picks a date as a user would, through the calendar's years, months and days.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>SignaturePad(label).DrawAsync(points)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Draws a stroke through points in the signature's 600 by 200 space and waits until the pad
                            has it. <code>UndoAsync</code>, <code>ClearAsync</code> and <code>TypeNameAsync(name)</code>
                            use its buttons and name field, and <code>IsSigned</code> says whether anything is drawn or
                            typed.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>Menu(trigger).ChooseAsync(path)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Clicks an item by its name, through the submenus named before it, and throws for a disabled
                            menu or item. <code>OpenAsync()</code> and <code>CloseAsync()</code> open and close the
                            menu, and <code>IsOpen</code> reads whether it's open.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>Hotkey(keys).PressAsync()</code></th>
                        <td>Runs a hotkey as a press of its keys would, and throws for a disabled one.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>Viewport(label)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Zooms with the viewport's buttons, and fits it with <code>FitAsync(ViewportFit.Page)</code>
                            or <code>ViewportFit.Width</code> from its menu; <code>Zoom</code> reads the zoom.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>QueryBuilder(label)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Adds and removes conditions and groups, finds a condition's fields by their labels with
                            <code>Condition(number, group)</code>, and sets a group's join with
                            <code>JoinAsync(QueryJoin.Or, group)</code>.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>PropertyGrid(label)</code></th>
                        <td>Finds fields inside the property grid by their labels, as the helpers above do.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>ContextMenu(name).On(key).ChooseAsync(path)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Opens the menu for the element with this <code>data-context-key</code>, or, without
                            <code>On</code>, for the rest of the area, and clicks an item as <code>Menu</code> does. The
                            name is the menu's <code>AriaLabel</code> and can be left out when the markup has only one
                            context menu.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>Tabs(name).SelectAsync(tab)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Selects a tab by its name; <code>Tabs</code> reads the tabs and <code>Selected</code> the
                            selected one. <code>Panel(tab)</code> scopes the helpers to that tab's panel, and
                            <code>Tabs()</code> with no name ignores tab lists nested in a panel.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>Tree(name)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Opens, closes, selects, checks, activates or drags a row by the texts along its path, with
                            <code>ExpandAsync</code>, <code>CollapseAsync</code>, <code>SelectAsync</code>,
                            <code>CheckAsync</code>, <code>ActivateAsync</code> and the drag steps
                            <code>DragBeforeAsync</code>, <code>DragAfterAsync</code> and <code>DragIntoAsync</code>.
                            <code>Texts</code> reads the rows that show, <code>Row(text)</code> finds the one row that
                            shows a text at any depth, and <code>Branch(path)</code> scopes the helpers to a row's
                            branch, with paths that start inside it. A
                            <code>TbTreeItem</code> works only inside a <code>TbTreeView</code>, so render the view
                            around it.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>TreeSelect(label)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Chooses an item by the texts along its path with <code>ChooseAsync</code>, checks and
                            unchecks items with <code>CheckAsync</code> and <code>UncheckAsync</code>, types in the
                            field's filter box with <code>FilterAsync</code>, and reads what the field shows with
                            <code>Text</code> and whether it's disabled with <code>Disabled</code>.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>DatePicker(label).ChooseAsync(date)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Types the date in the field's own format, and <code>Value</code> reads it.
                            <code>DateTimePicker</code> and <code>DateRangePicker</code> work the same way; the first
                            reads with <code>Value</code> and the second with <code>Range</code>.
                            <code>EnterAsync</code> types text as it is, such as a date the field can't read, to test
                            its error.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>Pagination(name).GoToPageAsync(number)</code></th>
                        <td>Goes to a page, and <code>Page</code> reads the current one.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>AccordionItem(header).ExpandAsync()</code></th>
                        <td>Opens the item and hands back its panel; <code>CollapseAsync()</code> closes it.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>Popconfirm(trigger).ConfirmAsync()</code></th>
                        <td>Presses the confirm button; <code>CancelAsync()</code> presses the cancel button.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>CommandPalette(name).ChooseAsync(command)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Runs a command in an open palette. <code>OpenAsync</code> opens it as its shortcut does,
                            <code>SearchAsync</code> types in its search box, <code>Results</code> reads the commands it
                            shows and <code>IsOpen</code> says whether it's open.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>Sortable(name).MoveAsync(item, index)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Moves an item to <code>index</code> through its handle's menu, and <code>Items</code> reads
                            the order. <code>DragBeforeAsync</code> and <code>DragAfterAsync</code> drop it on another
                            item, and <code>MoveToListAsync(item, list)</code> and <code>DragToListAsync</code> move it
                            to another list of its group. An item is named by the list's <code>ItemLabel</code>, or by
                            its text when the list has none.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>Slider(label).SetAsync(value)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Moves the slider as a drag does, landing on the nearest step within its range, and
                            <code>Value</code> reads it.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>Autocomplete(label).ChooseAsync(text, option)</code></th>
                        <td>Types the text and chooses a suggestion by its name.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>ColorPicker(label).ChooseAsync(color)</code></th>
                        <td>Types a color, as <code>#rrggbb</code> or <code>#rrggbbaa</code>, and commits it.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>FileUpload(label).ChooseAsync(paths)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Chooses the files at these paths. A file with a common extension gets the type a browser
                            gives it, such as <code>image/png</code> for a <code>.png</code>, and any other file an
                            empty type.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>Dialog(title).ChooseAsync(button)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Clicks an open dialog's button by its name, and <code>CancelAsync()</code> dismisses the
                            dialog as Escape does. Call it on the markup of the <code>TbRoot</code> that shows the
                            dialog; see <a href="guides/testing#bunit-dialogs">Test dialogs in bUnit</a>.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><code>Grid(name)</code></th>
                        <td>
                            Clicks or selects a row by a cell's text, with <code>ClickRowAsync</code> and
                            <code>SelectAsync</code>, sorts by a column with <code>SortAsync</code>, pages with
                            <code>GoToPageAsync</code>, filters a column with <code>FilterAsync</code> or picks a
                            filter's values with <code>FilterChoicesAsync</code>, resizes a column with
                            <code>ResizeColumnAsync</code>, moves a row to a place with <code>MoveRowAsync</code> or
                            drops it on another with <code>DragRowBeforeAsync</code> and <code>DragRowAfterAsync</code>,
                            and
                            opens or closes a tree grid's row with <code>ExpandAsync</code> and
                            <code>CollapseAsync</code>. In a grid that edits in batches,
                            <code>SaveChangesAsync</code> and <code>DiscardChangesAsync</code> press its Save changes and
                            Discard changes buttons. In a grid with <code>CellSelection</code>,
                            <code>SelectCellsAsync</code> selects a range, <code>SelectedCells</code> reads its text,
                            and in Playwright <code>CopyAsync</code> presses Ctrl+C and returns what was copied, with
                            no clipboard permission needed. <code>Rows</code> and <code>Selected</code> read the rows
                            shown and the selected ones, <code>RowCount</code> counts the rows shown,
                            <code>IsBusy</code> reads whether rows are loading, and <code>WaitForRowsAsync</code> waits
                            until they've loaded.
                            Finds a cell with <code>Cell</code>, and reads the column headers with <code>Columns</code>,
                            a column's total with <code>Total</code> and each group's totals with
                            <code>GroupTotals</code>. In a grid with <code>VirtualizeColumns</code>, these helpers see
                            only the columns drawn, so move focus to a column or scroll it into view first.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <CodeBlock Label="Driving fields in bUnit" Code="@BunitDrivers" />
        <ul>
            <li>As in Playwright, choosing refuses a disabled field.</li>
            <li>
                Choosing needs an interactive page, since a static select is a native one that posts with its form. The
                slider, autocomplete, color picker and file upload helpers throw on a static page for the same reason.
            </li>
            <li>
                A number field without a <code>Format</code> reads what was typed through its own change event, which
                bUnit's <code>Change</code> doesn't reach. <code>EnterAsync</code> types into either kind.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <TbText>
            Every field's helper reads the field's error messages: <code>Error</code> is the first one,
            <code>Errors</code> all of them in order, and <code>Invalid</code> whether the field is marked invalid.
            In Playwright they're <code>ErrorAsync()</code>, <code>ErrorsAsync()</code> and
            <code>InvalidAsync()</code>. <code>LeaveAsync()</code> moves focus out of the field, which raises its
            <code>OnBlur</code>. A text or number field's <code>EnterAsync</code> also leaves the field after typing,
            as a user moving on would.
        </TbText>
        <DocsCallout Title="Check a field's error in the field">
            A field's error is also announced in <code>TbRoot</code>'s live region. To check that an error has
            cleared, look in the field, not the whole page, as the field's helper's <code>Error</code> does.
        </DocsCallout>

        <DocsHeading Id="bunit-dialogs" Text="Test dialogs in bUnit" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" As="TextElement.H3">A dialog on its own</TbText>
        <TbText>
            <code>this.ShowDialogAsync&lt;TDialog, TResult&gt;()</code> opens a dialog as a page would, and
            <code>this.ShowDialogAsync&lt;TDialog&gt;()</code> opens one with no result. Each renders a
            <code>TbRoot</code> to host the dialog and opens it through <code>IDialogService</code> with the title,
            parameters and options you give. The result has three properties:
        </TbText>
        <ul>
            <li><code>Root</code>, to find what the dialog shows.</li>
            <li><code>Content</code>, the dialog's component.</li>
            <li><code>Reference</code>, to close the dialog or await its result.</li>
        </ul>
        <DocsCallout Tone="Tone.Warning" Title="One TbRoot per test">
            <code>ShowDialogAsync</code> renders one for you, and a second call in the same test opens its dialog in
            that same <code>TbRoot</code>. A test that renders its own, such as one beside the page it tests, calls
            <code>root.ShowDialogAsync&lt;TDialog&gt;()</code> on it instead, so the dialog shows once.
        </DocsCallout>
        <CodeBlock Label="Testing a dialog in bUnit" Code="@BunitDialog" />

        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" As="TextElement.H3">Dialogs a page opens</TbText>
        <TbText>
            <code>AddTrailblazor()</code> makes a <code>FakeDialogService</code> the <code>IDialogService</code>; get it
            from <code>Services</code>. Dialogs open as they otherwise would, and the service records each one in
            <code>Opened</code>, with its <code>Title</code>, <code>ContentType</code>, <code>Parameters</code> and
            <code>Options</code>, which for a confirm or alert include the options it was given.
        </TbText>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <code>Last&lt;TDialog, TResult&gt;()</code> finds the last dialog of a type, to check and close, and
                <code>Last&lt;TDialog&gt;()</code> finds one with no result. A title from the content's
                <code>DefaultTitle</code> is there only once a rendered <code>TbRoot</code> shows the dialog.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>LastConfirm()</code> finds the last confirm or alert; its <code>Message</code> is the text it
                shows.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>Answer</code> answers each confirm and alert as it opens: true confirms, false cancels, and null
                leaves it open.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>AnswerWith&lt;TDialog, TResult&gt;(result)</code> closes each <code>TDialog</code> with
                <code>result</code> as it opens, and <code>AnswerWithCancel&lt;TDialog, TResult&gt;()</code> cancels
                each one, or <code>AnswerWithCancel&lt;TDialog&gt;()</code> one with no result, so a click that awaits
                it finishes.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <TbText>
            Without an answer, a click whose handler awaits a dialog finishes only once the dialog closes, so:
        </TbText>
        <ol>
            <li>Start the click without awaiting it.</li>
            <li>Find the dialog and close it.</li>
            <li>Await the click.</li>
        </ol>
        <CodeBlock Label="Checking the dialogs a page opens" Code="@FakeDialogs" />
        <TbText>
            To use a dialog's own controls, render a <code>TbRoot</code> before the page, as a layout does. The dialog
            shows in the root's markup, so <code>root.Tb()</code> finds its fields, and
            <code>root.Tb().Dialog(title)</code> clicks its buttons by name or dismisses it as Escape does.
        </TbText>
        <CodeBlock Label="Using a dialog's controls in bUnit" Code="@PageDialogControls" />

        <DocsHeading Id="announcements" Text="Test what screen readers hear" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>
            Components tell screen readers about changes they can't see, such as a search's result count or a field's
            error, through live regions in <code>TbRoot</code> and each modal dialog. A test can check those messages
            too.
        </TbText>
        <ul>
            <li>
                In bUnit, <code>AddTrailblazor()</code> makes a <code>FakeAnnouncer</code> the
                <code>IAnnouncer</code>; get it from <code>Services</code>. Its <code>Polite</code> and
                <code>Assertive</code> lists hold every message, oldest first, even ones since withdrawn or cleared.
                The messages still reach the live regions, so a rendered <code>TbRoot</code> shows them too.
            </li>
            <li>
                In Playwright, <code>page.Tb().Announcements.ExpectAnnouncedAsync(text)</code> waits for a message
                that contains <code>text</code>.
                <code>Messages</code>, <code>Polite</code> and <code>Assertive</code> are locators for assertions. A
                message stays in its live region for seven seconds, unless five newer messages of its kind push it out
                first.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <CodeBlock Label="Checking what screen readers hear" Code="@Announcements" />

        <DocsHeading Id="promises" Text="What every component promises" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>
            These hold for every Trailblazor component, and Trailblazor's own tests check them, so your tests can rely
            on them.
        </TbText>
        <ul>
            <li>
                A value your page sets is never sent back through its <code>Changed</code> callback, such as
                <code>ValueChanged</code> or <code>OpenChanged</code>. A component may still report a value it had to
                correct or refuse: a data grid set to a page past the last one reports the last page, and a disabled
                select asked to open reports that it stayed closed.
            </li>
            <li>
                A disabled or read-only field never changes its value, whatever is clicked or typed in it.
            </li>
            <li>
                Once removed from the page, a component leaves nothing running: no handler on Trailblazor's services,
                no callback the browser can still make, and no timer that later calls your page.
            </li>
            <li>
                An element you found keeps working after the component renders again, so you can click a button twice
                without finding it a second time. Blazor keeps an event handler's id only while each render passes an
                equal delegate: the same method on the same object. Trailblazor's text and number fields don't use
                <code>@@bind</code>, so their boxes keep working too. Its other inputs, such as the autocomplete, date,
                color and slider fields, still do for now, so find those again before raising a second event. In your
                own components, <code>@@bind</code> and a lambda that captures a loop variable make a new delegate on
                every render: if background work renders the component after a test finds an element but before the
                test raises its event, bUnit throws "There is no event handler with ID". Pass a method, such as
                <code>@@onchange="OnNameChanged"</code>, instead.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" As="TextElement.H3">Check your own components</TbText>
        <TbText>
            <code>TbContract.VerifyAsync&lt;TComponent&gt;()</code> checks a component of your own, such as a field
            that wraps a Trailblazor one, against the same rules. Each rule renders it afresh in bUnit, and the call
            throws a <code>TbContractException</code> listing every rule it breaks. Use its setup to give the
            parameters the component needs, the services it injects, stand-ins for the JavaScript calls it makes
            itself, and two sample values for any type it binds other than <code>string</code>, <code>bool</code>,
            the numeric types, <code>DateOnly</code>, <code>TimeOnly</code>, <code>DateTime</code>,
            <code>DateTimeOffset</code>, <code>TimeSpan</code>, enums with two or more values and Trailblazor's own
            value types: date and slider ranges, the grid's state, sorts and filters, and a query builder's query.
        </TbText>
        <CodeBlock Label="Checking your own component" Code="@Contract" />

        <DocsHeading Id="playwright" Text="Drive components in Playwright" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>
            Reference <code>Trailblazor.Testing.Playwright</code>, once it's available to install, and
            <code>page.Tb()</code> drives components the way a person does. Each helper waits for its component to
            be ready before it clicks or types. A helper the <a href="guides/testing#bunit-fields">bUnit helpers</a>
            also have goes by the same name there, and the same ones can be found without a name.
        </TbText>
        <TbText>
            When a label is used more than once, as in an open dialog over a form or in every row of a grid, call
            <code>Tb()</code> on what holds the component you want, such as <code>dialog.Tb()</code> or
            <code>row.Tb()</code>, and the helpers look only inside it. Dialogs, toasts, announcements and the lists a
            field opens are still found anywhere on the page, so <code>row.Tb().Dialog("Remove order")</code> finds the
            dialog the row opened. That works only while the scope is still on the page: once it's gone, as when a
            dialog you scoped to has closed, use <code>page.Tb()</code> for them. For a page inside an iframe, use
            <code>page.FrameLocator(...).Tb()</code>.
        </TbText>
        <CodeBlock Label="Scoping the helpers" Code="@Scoped" />
        <div class="docs-api" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Playwright helpers">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Component</th>
                        <th scope="col">Found by</th>
                        <th scope="col">What the helper does</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var use in Helpers)
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="row">@use.Component</th>
                            <td>@use.FoundBy</td>
                            <td>@DocsText.Render(use.Does)</td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <DocsCallout Title="Fields that share a label">
            When a select, color picker, text field, number field or autocomplete shares its label with others, as in
            the rows of an editable list, pass a locator instead of the label, such as
            <code>page.Tb().Select(row.GetByLabel("Size"))</code>, or scope the helpers to the row with
            <code>row.Tb()</code>. In bUnit, <code>cut.Tb().Within(() =&gt; row)</code> scopes them the same way.
        </DocsCallout>
        <TbText>
            Check what a helper's action changes, such as a value shown elsewhere on the page, with Playwright's
            <code>Expect</code>, which retries until the page shows it.
        </TbText>
        <CodeBlock Label="A Playwright test" Code="@Playwright" />

        <DocsHeading Id="waiting" Text="Wait for the page" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>
            A page shows before its event handlers are attached, so a click that comes too soon does nothing. Before it
            clicks or types, Playwright waits for the element to be in the page, visible, still, enabled and able to take
            the pointer. It can't tell whether Blazor has wired the element up yet, or whether a component's script has
            attached, so wait for those yourself, or use the helpers, which do.
        </TbText>
        <DocsCallout Tone="Tone.Warning" Title="Clicks don't retry">
            Playwright's <code>Expect</code> assertions retry, but clicks and key presses don't, so wait first.
        </DocsCallout>
        <div class="docs-api" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="What to wait for">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Before using</th>
                        <th scope="col">Wait for</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">The page</th>
                        <td>
                            <code>page.Tb().InteractiveAsync()</code>, which waits for the
                            <code>[data-tb-interactive]</code> mark <code>TbRoot</code> renders once its dialogs,
                            toasts and announcements run interactively. When <code>Routes</code> is interactive, that's
                            when the page is. When interactivity is set page by page, the mark appears with the first
                            interactive session and stays through later navigation, so it can't tell you the next page
                            is ready. Put a marker such as
                            <code>data-interactive="@@RendererInfo.IsInteractive"</code> on each interactive page's
                            root instead, and wait for it after each navigation.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">A component</th>
                        <td>
                            <code>data-ready</code>, an empty attribute, on the element the table below names, such as
                            a tree, a data grid, a select's combobox, a tab list, a menu's trigger or a number field's
                            input. The helpers wait for it too, and it stays the same between versions. A component
                            with <code>AttachOnFirstUse</code>, such as every move handle in a sortable list or a data
                            grid, gets it only once it's focused, pointed at or clicked, and carries
                            <code>data-attach-on-use</code> until then. Wait for
                            <code>[data-ready], [data-attach-on-use]</code>, which says the page is interactive; if it
                            carries <code>data-attach-on-use</code>, focus it, then wait for <code>data-ready</code>.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">A data grid's rows</th>
                        <td>
                            <code>aria-busy</code> to clear from its table, which a grid with a
                            <code>DataProvider</code> sets while rows load.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">A change the app answers</th>
                        <td>
                            <code>data-commits</code> to change on the field's root, tab list, sortable list or data
                            grid; it counts the changes the app has answered. An app that changes a component's
                            <code>@@key</code> replaces it, and the new component counts from 0 again, so the count can
                            look unchanged after the app has answered. End the wait when the old element leaves the
                            page, too; the helpers stop on either.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">A dialog's answer</th>
                        <td>
                            The dialog to leave the page. While it fades out it stays there with
                            <code>data-leaving</code>, and the app gets the answer only once it has gone, so waiting
                            for it to be hidden ends too soon. A dialog on a static page never fades or leaves, so wait for
                            it to close instead.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">An accordion item opening or closing</th>
                        <td>
                            The item's <code>data-state</code> to read <code>open</code> or <code>closed</code>. The
                            header's <code>aria-expanded</code> changes first, and the item reads <code>opening</code> or
                            <code>closing</code> while the panel grows or shrinks, so waiting for
                            <code>aria-expanded</code> ends too soon. <code>ExpandAsync</code> and
                            <code>CollapseAsync</code> wait for <code>data-state</code>.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">The result of an action</th>
                        <td>
                            The result itself, asserted with <code>Expect</code>, which retries until it shows, even
                            when a click's handler is still running. Neither Blazor nor Playwright has a wait for a page
                            with nothing left to do.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <CodeBlock Label="Waiting for the page" Code="@Waiting" />
        <TbText>
            The components below set <code>data-ready</code> on the element listed once they're ready to use: those
            that rely on a script once it has attached, and a few others once the page is interactive. Any other
            component works as soon as the page is interactive.
        </TbText>
        <div class="docs-api" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Where components set data-ready">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Component</th>
                        <th scope="col">Sets <code>data-ready</code> on</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var example in ExampleCatalog.All.Where(example => example.Ready is not null))
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="row"><a href="components/@(example.Name)#testing">@example.Title</a></th>
                            <td>@example.Ready!.Element</td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <DocsHeading Id="slow-link" Text="Test over a slow link" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>
            On a fast machine every click and key reaches the server at once, so a test can pass even when the page
            drops a key pressed before the previous one was handled. <code>page.UseSlowLinkAsync(delay)</code> from
            <code>Trailblazor.Testing.Playwright</code> holds back every message the page sends to an interactive
            server app by <code>delay</code>, as a slow link would. Call it before the page opens the app. Trailblazor
            runs the keyboard tests for its select, slider, toggle group, tree, splitter and data grid both ways. Your own
            keyboard tests are worth running over a slow link too.
        </TbText>
        <CodeBlock Label="Opening a page over a slow link" Code="@SlowLinkPage" />

        <DocsHeading Id="time" Text="Time and motion" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>Some components wait on timers or transitions. Skip the wait rather than sleep through it.</TbText>
        <div class="docs-api" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Skipping waits">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">What waits</th>
                        <th scope="col">How to skip it</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">
                            Toasts, announcements and the <code>DebounceDelay</code> of an autocomplete with no
                            <code>Search</code>
                        </th>
                        <td>
                            In bUnit, register a <code>FakeTimeProvider</code> as the <code>TimeProvider</code> before
                            <code>AddTrailblazor()</code>, and advance it.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">A tooltip's delays, which run in the browser</th>
                        <td>Set its <code>ShowDelay</code> to zero, or use Playwright's <code>page.Clock</code>.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">Transitions</th>
                        <td>
                            In Playwright, open a page with <code>ReducedMotion = ReducedMotion.Reduce</code>, and they
                            finish at once. Use it only in the tests that need it: it also hides what happens while a
                            transition runs, such as a dialog that never finishes closing because its fade never ends.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <CodeBlock Label="Time and motion" Code="@TimeAndMotion" />

        <DocsHeading Id="finding" Text="Find components" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>
            Find components by role and accessible name rather than by CSS classes, which can change between versions.
        </TbText>
        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" As="TextElement.H3">By role</TbText>
        <div class="docs-api" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Roles">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Component</th>
                        <th scope="col">Role</th>
                        <th scope="col">Named by</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><th scope="row">Button</th><td><code>button</code></td><td>Its text</td></tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">Text field and other inputs</th>
                        <td>The input's own role</td>
                        <td>Its label, so <code>GetByLabel</code> finds it</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">Select and autocomplete</th>
                        <td><code>combobox</code> with <code>option</code>s</td>
                        <td>Its label</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">Menu</th>
                        <td><code>menu</code> of <code>menuitem</code>s</td>
                        <td>Its trigger</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr><th scope="row">Dialog</th><td><code>dialog</code></td><td>Its title</td></tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">Popover's panel</th>
                        <td><code>dialog</code></td>
                        <td>Its title or trigger</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">Tabs</th>
                        <td><code>tab</code>s with <code>tabpanel</code>s</td>
                        <td>Their text</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <DocsCallout Title="Find a select or autocomplete by its role">
            Its label also names its list of options, which <code>GetByLabel</code> would match as well. Use
            <code>GetByRole(AriaRole.Combobox, new() { Name = "Size" })</code>.
        </DocsCallout>

        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" As="TextElement.H3">By part</TbText>
        <TbText>
            Every component marks the parts that have no role of their own with a stable <code>data-part</code>
            attribute, such as a field's <code>[data-part=helper]</code> text or <code>[data-part=error]</code>
            message. Each component's page lists its parts.
        </TbText>
        <TbText>
            Announcements go into <code>TbRoot</code>'s <code>[data-part=announcer]</code>, or a modal dialog's own, as
            a <code>p</code> in <code>[data-part=polite]</code> or <code>[data-part=assertive]</code>. Several can be
            there at once, so find the one with your text.
        </TbText>

        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" As="TextElement.H3">By id</TbText>
        <TbText>
            An <code>id</code> you give a field names its control, as on a native input: <code>&lt;TbTextField
            id="email" /&gt;</code> puts <code>id="email"</code> on the input, and its label points to it, so
            <code>#email</code> reaches what the user types into.
        </TbText>
        <div class="docs-api" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Where an id goes">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Component</th>
                        <th scope="col">Its id goes on</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><th scope="row">Text field and other single fields</th><td>Its control</td></tr>
                    <tr><th scope="row">Checkbox and switch</th><td>Its box</td></tr>
                    <tr><th scope="row">Select</th><td>Its combobox</td></tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">Date range picker, range slider, checkbox group and radio group</th>
                        <td>Its root, since it has two or more controls</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr><th scope="row">A radio or checkbox in a group</th><td>Its input</td></tr>
                    <tr><th scope="row">Option</th><td>Itself</td></tr>
                    <tr><th scope="row">Tab</th><td>Its tab button, not its panel</td></tr>
                    <tr><th scope="row">Menu and popover</th><td>Its trigger, built in or your own</td></tr>
                    <tr><th scope="row">List item</th><td>Its link or button when it acts, otherwise the item</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <DocsCallout Tone="Tone.Warning" Title="A custom trigger's id">
            A custom trigger gets its id from <code>context.Attributes</code>, so put the id on the
            <code>TbMenu</code> or <code>TbPopover</code>, not on your button.
        </DocsCallout>
        <CodeBlock Label="Finding components" Code="@Locators" />

        <DocsHeading Id="recipes" Text="Recipes" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" As="TextElement.H3">A file upload opened from a menu or button</TbText>
        <TbText>
            A <code>Hidden</code> file upload that your own button or menu item opens still has its label, so
            <code>FileUpload(label).ChooseAsync(path)</code> gives it files in both packages. To check that the button
            opens the browser's file dialog, wait for the field's input to carry <code>data-ready</code>, then click
            the button inside Playwright's <code>RunAndWaitForFileChooserAsync</code> and set the files on the chooser.
        </TbText>
        <CodeBlock Label="A file upload opened from a button" Code="@HiddenUpload" />
        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" As="TextElement.H3">One tab's fields when every panel renders</TbText>
        <TbText>
            With <code>RenderAllPanels</code>, every tab's panel is in the page, so a field that each panel has matches
            more than once. <code>Tabs(name).Panel(tab)</code> scopes the helpers to that tab's panel. In Playwright,
            select the tab first, since the other panels are hidden; bUnit reads hidden panels too.
        </TbText>
        <CodeBlock Label="One tab's fields" Code="@OneTab" />
        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" As="TextElement.H3">Output that settles a render later</TbText>
        <TbText>
            Some output changes a render after the event that causes it, such as a count the app updates once a save
            it awaits has finished. A read made straight after the event can still see the old output, so retry the
            check: <code>cut.WaitForAssertion</code> retries it after each render in bUnit, and Playwright's
            <code>Expect</code> retries until the page shows it.
        </TbText>
        <CodeBlock Label="Output that settles later" Code="@Settles" />

        <DocsHeading Id="related" Text="Related" Class="docs-section" />
        <ul>
            <li>
                <a href="guides/migrating-tests">Migrating tests</a>: moving a suite over from another component
                library.
            </li>
            <li><a href="guides/browser">Browser</a>: the fake browser service and what it records.</li>
            <li><a href="guides/announcements">Announcements</a>: the live regions tests read.</li>
            <li><a href="guides/telemetry">Telemetry</a>: counting what people use.</li>
            <li>
                Each component's page ends with a Testing section naming its roles, parts and readiness, such as the
                <a href="components/data-grid#testing">data grid's</a>.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <GuideFooter Page="GetType()" />
    </ChildContent>
</DocsArticle>

@code {
    private sealed record Helper(string Component, string FoundBy, string Does);

    private static readonly Helper[] Helpers =
    [
        new("Select", "Its label", "`Select(label).ChooseAsync(options)` chooses by option names, whether the select "
            + "renders as Trailblazor's list or, on a static page, as a native `<select>`. `UnchooseAsync(options)` "
            + "unchooses options in a multiple select, `ClearAsync()` clears a `Clearable` one, `TextAsync()` reads "
            + "what it shows, `ChosenAsync()` reads the chosen options, and `OptionsAsync()` reads them all. "
            + "`OptionAsync(name)` reads whether an option is disabled and chosen, and `DisabledAsync()` reads the "
            + "select. `OpenAsync()` and `CloseAsync()` open and close its list, and `IsOpenAsync()` reads whether "
            + "it's open."),
        new("Text field", "Its label", "`TextField(label).EnterAsync(text)` types the text and waits until the app "
            + "has it, and `TextAsync()` reads what the field shows."),
        new("Checkbox and switch", "Its label", "`Checkbox(label).CheckAsync()` and `UncheckAsync()` wait until the "
            + "app has the value, and `CheckedAsync()` reads it. `Switch(label)` works the same way."),
        new("Checkbox group", "Its label", "`CheckboxGroup(label).CheckAsync(options)` and `UncheckAsync(options)` "
            + "change options by name, and `CheckedAsync()` reads the checked ones."),
        new("Radio group", "Its label", "`RadioGroup(label).ChooseAsync(option)` chooses an option by name, and "
            + "`ChosenAsync()` reads it."),
        new("Toggle group", "Its label, or its `AriaLabel`",
            "`ToggleGroup(label).ChooseAsync(items)` presses items by name, "
            + "`UnchooseAsync(items)` releases them, and `ChosenAsync()` reads the pressed ones."),
        new("List", "Its `aria-label`", "`List(name).ClickAsync(item)` clicks an item by its text, and "
            + "`ItemsAsync()` reads them."),
        new("Autocomplete", "Its label",
            "`Autocomplete(label).ChooseAsync(text, option)` types and picks an option."),
        new("List box", "Its label", "`ListBox(label).ChooseAsync(options)` chooses by option names, "
            + "`ChosenAsync()` reads the chosen ones, and `OptionsAsync()` reads them all. In a multiple list box, "
            + "`UnchooseAsync(options)` unchooses some and `ClearAsync()` unchooses all. `OptionAsync(name)` and "
            + "`DisabledAsync()` read an option and the list box."),
        new("Color picker", "Its label", "`ColorPicker(label).ChooseAsync(color)` picks a color."),
        new("File upload", "Its label", "`FileUpload(label).ChooseAsync(paths)` chooses files."),
        new("Date picker", "Its label", "`DatePicker(label).ChooseAsync(date)` types the date in the field's format "
